\section*{Method of Multiplication.}

751.] If we make the transient current pass every time that
 the magnet passes through the zero point, and always so as
 to increase the velocity of the magnet, then, if $\theta_{1}, \theta_{2}$, \&c. are
 the successive elongations,

$$\begin{align*}
& \theta_{2}=-K Q-e^{-\lambda} \theta_{1},  \tag{33}\\
& \theta_{3}=+K Q-e^{-\lambda} \theta_{2} . \tag{34}
\end{align*}$$


The ultimate value to which the elongation tends after a great
 many vibrations is found by putting $\theta_{n}=-\theta_{n-1}$, whence we find

$$\begin{equation*}
\theta= \pm \frac{1}{1-e^{-\lambda}} K Q . \tag{35}
\end{equation*}$$


If $\lambda$ is small, the value of the ultimate elongation may be
 large, but since this involves a long continued experiment, and a
 careful determination of $\lambda$, and since a small error in $\lambda$ intro
duces a large error in the determination of $Q$, this method is
 rarely useful for numerical determination, and should be re
served for obtaining evidence of the existence or non-existence
 of currents too small to be observed directly.


